      "latency_ms": 10180.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 529,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula:** $H_2O$\n* **Structure:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. \n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in oceans, rivers, and rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, and glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, and humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n* **Ice Expansion:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, allowing aquatic life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Essence of Life:** All known life depends on water. The human body is made up of about 60% water, and cells require a watery environment to carry out chemical reactions.\n* **Earth’s Life Support:** Often called the \"Blue Planet,\" about 71% of Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans. \n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed system known as the hydrological cycle: it evaporates from the surface, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows into rivers and oceans.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the fuel, medium, and habitat for life on Earth."
